<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Further Pomerania</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Farther Pomerania, Hinterpommern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> is the eastern part of historical Pomerania, lying east of the Oder along the southern Baltic coast, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a> (Słupsk), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koszalin">Köslin</a> and Stargard. It was ruled by branches of the Griffin dukes, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a> in 1653 and became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/further-pomerania.jpg" alt="Further Pomerania" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrzej Otrębski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kolobrzeg_latarnia_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Farther <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, also called Hinder Pomerania, Rear Pomerania or Eastern Pomerania, is a subregion of historic Pomerania in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It lies mostly within the West Pomeranian Voivodeship, with its easternmost parts in the Pomeranian Voivodeship. It stretches roughly from the Oder in the west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> in the east, and Polish historiography takes the river Łeba as the eastern limit. Its four cities are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kołobrzeg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koszalin">Koszalin</a>, Stargard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Słupsk</a>. The name Pomerania comes from the Slavic po more, land at the sea. By the 16th century the name had come to cover the areas east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 960s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> became part of the emerging Polish state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, and around 1000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a> founded the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolobrzeg">Kołobrzeg</a>. Farther Pomerania emerged as a subdivision of the Duchy of Pomerania in the partition of 1532, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-stettin">Pomerania-Stettin</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stettin-1653">Treaty of Stettin</a> of 1653 divided the duchy between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>, and Farther Pomerania became the Brandenburg-Prussian Province of Pomerania. After the reorganisation of the Prussian Province of Pomerania in 1815 it was administered as the Regierungsbezirk Köslin, and in 1938 the northern part of the dissolved Grenzmark Posen-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a> was merged into it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> in 1919 awarded the bulk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, with minor parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slupsk">Stolp</a>, Lauenburg and Bütow districts, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, while most of Farther Pomerania stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Nazi Germany set up the Stalag II-D prisoner-of-war camp at Stargard in 1939 and a camp for kidnapped Polish children at Połczyn-Zdrój. In 1945 death marches of prisoners from the Stutthof concentration camp and from prisoner-of-war camps passed through the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat in 1945 the region was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> under the border decisions confirmed at the Potsdam Conference. German civilians left or were removed from the territories east of the Oder-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nysa">Neisse</a> line, and many new inhabitants came from central Poland and from eastern borderlands incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Before 1999 the area was administered as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Szczecin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koszalin">Koszalin</a> and Słupsk Voivodeships. Under the reform effective 1 January 1999 it was divided between the West Pomeranian Voivodeship, which took about two thirds, and the Pomeranian Voivodeship. The main regional museums stand in Słupsk, Stargard, Koszalin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darlowo">Darłowo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Farther Pomerania” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Peace of Westphalia of 1648 divided the duchy: Sweden kept Western Pomerania with Szczecin, Stralsund, Usedom and Wolin, and Brandenburg received <b>Farther Pomerania</b>; the Treaty of Stettin of 1653 fixed the border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…electors brought Cleves, Mark and Ravensberg in 1614 and the Duchy of Prussia in 1618, and the Peace of Westphalia in 1648 added <b>Farther Pomerania</b>, Halberstadt, Minden and the expectancy of Magdeburg, so that a minor electorate became the core of a composite state stretching across northern Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1368 a new partition of Pomerania-Wolgast gave the lands of <b>Further Pomerania</b>, east of the Świna and Oder, to Duke Bogislaw V, who ruled from Stolp (Słupsk).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-stolp">Duchy of Pomerania-Stolp</a></li></ul></section>
